<details
  class="font-sans max-w-[760px] mx-auto mt-[14px] px-[22px] [&_summary]:cursor-pointer [&_summary]:text-primary [&_summary]:text-[13.5px] [&_summary]:w-max [&_summary]:font-medium [&_summary]:block [&[open]_.arrow]:rotate-180"
>
  <summary>New here? How to read this <span class="arrow inline-block transition-transform">&darr;</span></summary>
  <div
    class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-[10px_18px] mt-2 text-[14px] font-serif leading-[1.6]"
  >
    <p>
      cupel sorts stories by the <b>wish</b> they pay out, not by plot. Each
      <b>engine</b> works the same way: the reader is stuck, something releases
      them, and that release is the payoff they came for (e.g.
      <i>repricing</i> = dismissed, then revalued).
    </p>
    <ul class="[&_li]:my-[5px] mt-2">
      <li>
        <b>slot</b> — an engine&rsquo;s test has 2&ndash;3 slots; a work earns
        the tag only when <i>every</i> slot fills with a real quote. No
        interpretation, just citation (the Bechdel-test move).
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>&#10003; slot-proven</b> vs <b>&tilde; reviewed</b> &mdash; &#10003;
        has a verbatim-cited dossier behind it; &tilde; is asserted from
        secondary sources, not yet slot-checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>spine</b> vs <b>rider</b> / <b>also-runs</b> &mdash; whether the
        engine is the work&rsquo;s core, or a secondary thread bolted on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>the counterfeit</b> &mdash; every engine has a dark twin: the same
        machine sold as a recruitment pitch, handing you the payoff
        <i>identity</i> while skipping the costly <i>work</i> that earns it
        (the badge minus its backing).
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>texture vs engine</b> &mdash; what a work delivers as
        <i>experience</i> (awe, dread, spectacle) is <b>texture</b>; what
        people consume the work <i>for</i> is the <b>engine</b>. Awe in
        <i>Jurassic Park</i> rides on order/legibility; texture is what the
        work feels like, not what drives consumption.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</details>
